Feature: a brightness setting, as a power on the game's display gamma ramp

Applies to: rexglue-sdk nightly-20260921-923c1a59 (thirdparty/rexglue-sdk), after 0013
Apply with: git -C thirdparty/rexglue-sdk apply ../../patches/rexglue-sdk/0015-gamma-ramp-power.patch
Status:     local patch (a port feature; not an upstream bug)

WHAT
  The port's Options screen has a Brightness row (it replaces the original's
  TV "Calibration" screen, which only showed a grey scale). The game sets a
  256-entry display gamma ramp (D3D -> the DC_LUT registers); the command
  processor keeps the table and applies it to the picture at every swap.

FIX
  GPU flag gamma_ramp_power (double, 1.0 = the game's own ramp, below 1
  brighter, above 1 darker; hot reload). The Vulkan swap uploads the
  256-entry table with the power applied to each 10-bit channel
  (CommandProcessor::GammaRampEntryWithPower, inline in the header so the
  port's native renderer applies the same curve to the same table), and a
  change of the flag re-uploads it at the next swap. The PWL ramp is left
  alone (the game uses the 256-entry table).

HOW FOUND
  docs/findings/09 s.5 (the ramp), the swap's ramp upload in
  VulkanCommandProcessor::IssueSwap.

--- a/include/rex/graphics/command_processor.h
+++ b/include/rex/graphics/command_processor.h
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 
 #pragma once
 
+#include <cmath>
 #include <atomic>
 #include <cstring>
 #include <functional>
@@ -170,6 +171,24 @@
   // skipped because of the GPU flag draw_every_nth_frame. Read per draw (and
   // at the swap), so a change of the flag takes effect at once.
   bool CurrentFrameSkipped() const;
+  // Mind over Recomp (patch 0015): the picture's brightness, as a power on
+  // the game's display gamma ramp (GPU flag gamma_ramp_power: 1 = the game's
+  // own ramp, below 1 brighter, above 1 darker). Applied where the ramp is
+  // uploaded for the swap; a host renderer drawing the game applies the same.
+  static double GammaRampPower();
+  // One 256-entry table value (DC_LUT_30_COLOR bits: 10-bit blue 0-9, green
+  // 10-19, red 20-29) with the power applied to each channel. Inline: the
+  // host renderer (outside the GPU plugin) uses the same curve.
+  static uint32_t GammaRampEntryWithPower(uint32_t entry, double power) {
+    if (power == 1.0) return entry;
+    uint32_t out = entry & ~UINT32_C(0x3FFFFFFF);
+    for (uint32_t shift = 0; shift < 30; shift += 10) {
+      const double v = double((entry >> shift) & 0x3FF) / 1023.0;
+      const uint32_t c = uint32_t(std::lround(std::pow(v, power) * 1023.0));
+      out |= (c < 0x3FFu ? c : 0x3FFu) << shift;
+    }
+    return out;
+  }
 
   virtual void PrepareForWait();
   virtual void ReturnFromWait();
